    <h2>1. What is a Function?</h2>
    <p><b>Function = A Block of Code That Does One Job.</b> You write it once, give it a name, and then you can use it again and again by calling its name.</p>
    <p>Example: A TV remote has a button for Power. You do not open the TV every time. You just press the button. A function is the same - a button for your code.</p>
    <div class="note"><b>Why Use Functions?</b> 1. No repeat code - Write once, use 100 times. 2. Easy to fix - Fix in one place. 3. Clean code - Small blocks, easy to read.</div>
    <h2>2. LIVE DEMO - Function Calculator</h2>
    <p>Type two numbers. Each button calls a different function:</p>
    <div class="demo-card">
      <h3 id="demoAnswer" style="margin:0">Answer: -</h3>
      <p style="color:#64748b;margin:8px 0">Each button calls one function</p>
      <input class="demo-input" id="fNum1" type="number" placeholder="First Number - e.g. 10" value="10">
      <input class="demo-input" id="fNum2" type="number" placeholder="Second Number - e.g. 5" value="5">
      <button class="btn btn-js" onclick="callAdd()">Add ➕</button>
      <button class="btn btn-next" onclick="callSubtract()">Subtract ➖</button>
      <button class="btn" onclick="callMultiply()">Multiply ✖️</button>
      <button class="btn btn-js" onclick="callGreet()">Say Hello 👋</button>
    </div>
    <h2>3. How to Make a Function? - 2 Steps</h2>
    <p><b>Step 1 - Define:</b> Create the function. <b>Step 2 - Call:</b> Use the function.</p>
    <div class="code-box"><code>
<span class="comment" style="color:#94a3b8">// Step 1 - DEFINE - Create the function</span><br>
<span class="text">function sayHello() {</span><br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="text">console.log("Hello! Welcome to My Website.");</span><br>
<span class="text">}</span><br><br>
<span class="comment" style="color:#94a3b8">// Step 2 - CALL - Use the function</span><br>
<span class="text">sayHello(); // Output: Hello! Welcome to My Website.</span><br>
<span class="text">sayHello(); // You can call it again and again</span><br>
<span class="text">sayHello(); // 3rd time - Same code, no repeat writing</span>
    </code></div>
    <p><b>Important:</b> If you only define a function but never call it, it will never run. Defining is like writing a recipe. Calling is like cooking the food.</p>
    <h2>4. Parameters and Arguments</h2>
    <p><b>Parameter = Empty Box in Function Definition.</b> <b>Argument = Real Value You Send When You Call.</b></p>
    <div class="code-box"><code>
<span class="comment" style="color:#94a3b8">// name is a PARAMETER - Empty box</span><br>
<span class="text">function greet(name) {</span><br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="text">console.log("Hello, " + name + "!");</span><br>
<span class="text">}</span><br><br>
<span class="comment" style="color:#94a3b8">// "Ali" is an ARGUMENT - Real value</span><br>
<span class="text">greet("Ali"); // Hello, Ali!</span><br>
<span class="text">greet("Ahmed"); // Hello, Ahmed!</span><br>
<span class="text">greet("Sara"); // Hello, Sara!</span>
    </code></div>
    <p>Same function, different results. That is the power of parameters.</p>
    <h2>5. Multiple Parameters</h2>
    <div class="code-box"><code>
<span class="text">function add(a, b) {</span><br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="text">console.log(a + b);</span><br>
<span class="text">}</span><br><br>
<span class="text">add(10, 20); // 30</span><br>
<span class="text">add(5, 5); // 10</span><br>
<span class="text">add(100, 200); // 300</span>
    </code></div>
    <h2>6. return - Get Answer Back From Function</h2>
    <p>Sometimes a function does work and gives an answer back. That answer is sent with <code>return</code>.</p>
    <div class="code-box"><code>
<span class="text">function add(a, b) {</span><br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="text">return a + b; // Send answer back</span><br>
<span class="text">}</span><br><br>
<span class="text">let result = add(10, 20); // result = 30</span><br>
<span class="text">console.log(result); // 30</span><br><br>
<span class="text">let total = add(5, 5) + add(10, 10); // 10 + 20 = 30</span><br>
<span class="text">console.log(total);</span>
    </code></div>
    <div class="note"><b>Difference:</b> <code>console.log()</code> only shows the value on screen. <code>return</code> gives the value back, so you can store it in a variable and use it later. For real work, use <code>return</code>.</div>
    <h2>7. Function vs No Function - See the Difference</h2>
    <table>
      <tr><th>Without Function</th><th>With Function</th></tr>
      <tr><td>Write same code 10 times for 10 buttons</td><td>Write code 1 time, call it for 10 buttons</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Fix a bug in 10 places</td><td>Fix a bug in 1 place</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Long, messy code</td><td>Short, clean code</td></tr>
    </table>
    <h2>8. Complete Example - Greet + Add</h2>
    <div class="code-box"><code>
<span class="tag">&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;</span><br>
<span class="tag">&lt;html&gt;</span><br>
<span class="tag">&lt;body&gt;</span><br><br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="tag">&lt;h1</span> <span class="attr">id</span>=<span class="val">"result"</span><span class="tag">&gt;</span><span class="text">Result Here</span><span class="tag">&lt;/h1&gt;</span><br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="tag">&lt;input</span> <span class="attr">id</span>=<span class="val">"name"</span> <span class="attr">type</span>=<span class="val">"text"</span> <span class="attr">placeholder</span>=<span class="val">"Your Name"</span><span class="tag">&gt;</span><br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="tag">&lt;button</span> <span class="attr">onclick</span>=<span class="val">"showGreet()"</span><span class="tag">&gt;</span><span class="text">Greet Me</span><span class="tag">&lt;/button&gt;</span><br><br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="tag">&lt;script&gt;</span><br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="text">function greet(name) {</span><br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="text">return "Hello, " + name + "! Welcome!";</span><br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="text">}</span><br><br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="text">function showGreet() {</span><br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="text">let n = document.getElementById("name").value;</span><br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="text">let message = greet(n); // Call function, get answer</span><br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="text">document.getElementById("result").innerHTML = message;</span><br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="text">}</span><br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="tag">&lt;/script&gt;</span><br>
<span class="tag">&lt;/body&gt;</span><br>
<span class="tag">&lt;/html&gt;</span>
    </code></div>
    <h2>9. Homework</h2>
    <p>In Playground, build this:</p>
    <p>1. Function <code>square(num)</code> - return num * num. Call it with 5, 10, 20<br>2. Function <code>isAdult(age)</code> - if age &gt;= 18 return true, else return false<br>3. Function <code>calculateTotal(price, quantity)</code> - return price * quantity. Use it for 3 different products</p>
    <div class="box"><b>Remember:</b> Function = Reusable block of code | Define = Create function | Call = Use function - functionName() | Parameter = Empty box in definition | Argument = Real value when calling | return = Send answer back | console.log = Only show, return = Give back to use</div>
    <p><b>Next Step:</b> <a href="/lesson27">Lesson 27 - Arrays</a> - Learn how to store many values in one variable. List of names, list of prices.</p>
